{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-5-5","id":"msg_011CfWndobuajt8DamA1s6gv","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_018kwBpJ5yytoukrYbkVZZXE","name":"StructuredOutput","input":{"summary":"I wrote the three missing files in my write scope. No other file was changed. Writing them also created the `design/` folder, which did not exist before. I did not touch the CLI; it was already corrected (sha256 fe8f4080…).\n\n1. **`design/implementation-design.md`** (sha256 3cacf757…). This is the design for the CLI itself, based on `specification/project-conformance.md` (9d02e5fb…).\n   - It defines `generated/hello-world.mjs` as a single file with no imports and one line: `console.log(\"Hello, world!\");`.\n   - It explains why that line produces exactly the required 14 bytes, why a line ending is always a single LF, and why the script must not call `process.exit()` (that can cut off output written to a pipe).\n   - It checks the current file against the design: it matches. It records the history tied to each version of the file: the failing baseline run on the old version (result 6514754a), the one-line fix (e897db22), and the passing run on the current version (db34d3cf). It also says what would make the design out of date.\n   - It names the component test, the UAT test and both fixed commands, and maps every conformance requirement R-01..R-14 to a part of the design.\n\n2. **`design/test-design.md`** (sha256 1995fa7e…). This is the test design.\n   - It states what the CLI must do: exit status 0, and stdout exactly `Hello, world!\\n`.\n   - It checks both supplied tests line by line against the original instructions. Both run the real CLI and use strict equality for the checks.\n   - It lists which faults the tests catch, and cites the baseline run where both tests failed on the missing comma. That shows the tests actually detect a wrong greeting and do not pass by default.\n   - It specifies both fixed commands and how to read the test counts under both reporter formats. It requires a passing test from each file, not just a total of 2 passes, and spells out the original rule: exit 0, passes at least the expected count, and the plan satisfied.\n   - It lists where the tests fall short (R-T1..R-T4).\n\n3. **`test-execution-plan.json`** (sha256 e940dcf2…).\n   - It has the fields the original requires: `command` \"node\", the exact `args`, `expectedTestPassCount` 2, `expectedStdoutMatch` \"fail 0\" (no exact pass count pinned), and `assertedReturnValue` \"Hello, world!\".\n   - It adds: a direct CLI check (`node generated/hello-world.mjs`, exit 0, exact 14-byte stdout and its hash); the two test names that must pass, one per file; a mapping to both fixed command IDs; the acceptance rule; the fields recorded run evidence must contain; the hashes of the input files; and the baseline → fix → current history.\n   - It states that the plan only declares expectations and does not decide which commands run.\n\n**My own checks (informative only, not recorded evidence):**\n- On local Node v24.7.0, command 1 printed exactly the 14 required bytes and exited 0, and command 2 had 2 passes, 0 failures and exit 0.\n- \"fail 0\" appears in both the default and TAP report formats.\n- Running outside the project root gives `MODULE_NOT_FOUND` and exit 1.\n- I applied the plan's acceptance rule to the recorded test output: it accepts the run on the current CLI and rejects the baseline run.\n- The original source, oracle, witness contract, both tests, the CLI and the conformance file all still match their recorded hashes.\n- I created no temporary files; all checks used pipes or in-memory processing.","gaps":["None of the seven obligations is closed. Each of the three new files needs a record of it being written, plus an independent assessment by a different actor/call. My own checks are not acceptance.","complete-original@5 needs all four written files, current recorded runs of both fixed commands, and 